{"causes":["The rise of Greek philosophical thought in the 5th century BCE influenced the moral themes in Alcmeonis.","The tradition of epic poetry established by Homer created a framework for later poets like Alcmeon.","Societal emphasis on ethics and morality during the Classical era shaped the content and focus of the poem."],"effects":["Alcmeonis influenced subsequent Greek literature, particularly in its exploration of moral themes and causality.","The poem contributed to the philosophical discourse on fate and free will, impacting later thinkers like Plato and Aristotle.","It served as a precursor to narrative techniques that emphasize character development based on moral choices in Western literature."],"relationships":[{"sourceSlug":"greek-philosophy","sourceName":"Greek Philosophy","verb":"INFLUENCES","targetSlug":"alcmeonis","targetName":"Alcmeonis","context":"The themes of Alcmeonis were heavily influenced by contemporary Greek philosophical thought, particularly regarding ethics and morality."},{"sourceSlug":"homeric-epics","sourceName":"Homeric Epics","verb":"CREATES","targetSlug":"alcmeonis","targetName":"Alcmeonis","context":"Alcmeonis was created in the tradition of epic poetry established by the works of Homer, drawing on similar narrative techniques."},{"sourceSlug":"plato","sourceName":"Plato","verb":"SUCCEEDS","targetSlug":"alcmeonis","targetName":"Alcmeonis","context":"Plato's exploration of morality in dialogues can be traced back to themes present in Alcmeonis."},{"sourceSlug":"classical-greece","sourceName":"Classical Greece","verb":"OCCURS_IN","targetSlug":"classical-greece","targetName":"Classical Greece","context":"Alcmeonis emerged during the Classical period of Greece, reflecting the cultural and intellectual currents of its time."},{"sourceSlug":"aristotle","sourceName":"Aristotle","verb":"INFLUENCES","targetSlug":"alcmeonis","targetName":"Alcmeonis","context":"The moral and ethical inquiries of Aristotle were influenced by the themes explored in Alcmeonis."}],"places":[{"name":"Athens, Greece","role":"Cultural center"},{"name":"Corinth, Greece","role":"Historical context"},{"name":"Delphi, Greece","role":"Religious significance"}],"texts":[{"title":"Wikipedia: Alcmeonis","type":"Reference article","slug":"alcmeonis"}],"externalLinks":["https://www.wikidata.org/wiki/Q1280294","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Alcmeonis"],"tags":["epic poem","Epic Poetry & Mythology","cause-and-effect"],"thumbnailUrl":"","quote":"","legacySummary":"","summary":"The Alcmeonis (Greek: Ἀλκμαιωνίς, Alkmaiōnis) is a lost ancient Greek epic poem dealing with the myth of Alcmaeon — the hero who killed his mother Eriphyle to avenge his father Amphiaraus (the seer who was killed in the battle of the Seven Against Thebes after his wife Eriphyle had bribed to reveal his hiding place) — and is one of the poems of the Greek Epic Cycle, the corpus of epic poems by various ancient Greek authors that supplemented and extended the Homeric narrative of the Trojan War and the myths of the heroic age. The Alcmeonis survives only in fragments — a few lines preserved in quotations by Athenaeus and other ancient authors — and its authorship, date, and exact scope are uncertain, though ancient sources attribute it to Abas or to anonymous Corinthian authorship, and it is generally dated to the 7th or early 6th century BCE.

The myth of Alcmaeon — the matricide hero, the subsequent madness (Erinyes-driven) visited on him for killing his mother, his wandering in search of purification, and his eventual death — was one of the most important mythological complexes of ancient Greece, explored by Sophocles (his lost Alcmaeon plays), Euripides (Alcmaeon in Psophidis and Alcmaeon in Corinth — the latter the last play he composed before his death), and Callimachus. The story of the Epigoni (the sons of the Seven Against Thebes, who successfully sacked Thebes in the second expedition) and of Alcmaeon's subsequent career as a wandering hero persecuted by the Erinyes for matricide were major subjects of the cyclic epics beyond the Alcmeonis itself.

The Alcmeonis is significant as evidence for the Greek Epic Cycle's broader scope — the cyclic epics treated not only the Trojan War mythology but the full range of the heroic age myths (Heracles, Theban cycle, Argonautic cycle), demonstrating the comprehensive narrative ambition of the early Greek epic tradition. Its loss, alongside the other cyclic epics (Cypria, Aethiopis, Little Iliad, Iliupersis, Nostoi, Telegony), is one of the major losses of ancient Greek literature.","subjects":["Ancient Greek Literature","Ancient Era","Greek Epic Cycle","Greek Mythology","Theban Cycle","Lost Literature","Classical Greece","Epic Poetry"],"frameworks":["STRUCTURAL_ANALYSIS","CAUSE_AND_EFFECT"],"historicalSignificance":{"significanceScore":7,"significanceNarrative":"Alcmeonis significantly shaped the literary and philosophical discourse of its time, influencing major thinkers like Plato and Aristotle, who grappled with similar themes of morality and causality. Its exploration of ethical dilemmas and human choices resonated throughout Western literature, impacting narrative forms for centuries.","significanceCategory":"regional"},"_editLog":[]}
